WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:05.779
we've all fantasized about jamming in
annoying person's

00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:10.720
cell phone. Mexican free-tailed bats have
figured out how to turn that dream

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.730
into a reality. They jam each other's bat
signals

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.100
but not because they're trying to turn
down the volume they're just trying to

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.699
steal each other's meals.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:23.590
These bats hang out in colonies that can
number in the millions

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.080
but living with so many relatives can
lead to conflict.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.600
When the colony swarms out to feed night
competition is fierce.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.030
To get an edge, the bats jam the sensory
equipment of nearby buddies

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.420
then they can swoop in and steal the
meal for themselves.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.559
Echolocation is basically biological
sonar.

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:46.640
First you emit a sound, then you listen
for an echo. 

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.950
Which tells you the position and speed
of things around you.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.500
And just like sonar, bats can all listen
in on the same channel

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.120
at a frequency mostly above the range
of human hearing.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.579
This is the sound

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:05.500
for free-tail bat getting closer and
closer to its target

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.600
 

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.840
Often a flying insect.  While still an orderly sound

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.450
like a normal echolocation call, the audio pulses

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.249
speed up because the bat needs to get
more information quickly

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:22.829
as it nears its prey. Unfortunately this
"feeding buzz"

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:26.499
also lets nearby hunting bats know that a
competitor

00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:29.859
is closing in on dinner. Using
triangulation

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:35.060
Aaron Corcoran and William Conner were able to show that when nearby bats hear the buzz

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.899
they swerve from their flight paths and
hone in on the hunting bat.

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:43.630
As these interfering bats close in,
they actually try to disrupt the hunt

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:49.979
by emitting a jamming call of their
own. 

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:55.390
These vocalizations swoop up and down like a siren--covering all the

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:00.120
frequencies made in the feeding buzz. The jamming call

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.770
causes the hunting bat to lose track of its prey and the jamming bat 

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.480
takes the opportunity to snap it up.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.520
We don't know whether other types of 
bats are using jamming to steal food

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.500
from one another

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.650
but now researchers know to look out for
this new adaptation

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.100
in highly competitive feeding
environments.

